WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:14.449
The first part of preparation is identifying the critical information

2
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.309
that needs protection and avoiding any single point of failure.

3
00:00:18.639 --> 00:00:21.729
This means that if we have something particularly important

4
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.120
but it is protected by just one door,

5
00:00:24.129 --> 00:00:26.549
we create multiple layers of protection to

6
00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:28.909
reduce the likelihood of a successful attack.

7
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.389
We will talk more later about the principle of defense in depth, but like a fortress.

8
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.369
The more layers of defense we have the more difficult it

9
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.150
will be for Attackers who are trying to break through.

10
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.520
It is important to train staff in incident

11
00:00:43.529 --> 00:00:45.819
response so that everyone knows what to do.

12
00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.080
Training can include simulations and scenarios.

13
00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.520
So teams can practice their response and learn to

14
00:00:51.529 --> 00:00:55.450
coordinate communication among the different stakeholders of the organization

15
00:00:55.939 --> 00:00:58.400
that includes colleagues, superiors,

16
00:00:58.409 --> 00:01:01.540
the owners of the information and customers as well.

17
00:01:01.729 --> 00:01:04.330
We need to consider what types of communication will be

18
00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:08.279
available because we cannot communicate the same information to everyone.

19
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.389
Some material will be confidential and some will be useful only

20
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.970
to certain people and not to the press or outside individuals.

21
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.589
When it comes to detection and analysis, we need to monitor the attack vectors,

22
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.019
how the attack was made and what technology was used.

23
00:01:25.489 --> 00:01:28.580
It is important to standardize the incident documentation

24
00:01:28.699 --> 00:01:30.190
because in a group of people,

25
00:01:30.199 --> 00:01:34.059
each will have their own idea of how to record activities and procedures

26
00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:38.940
for the consistency of the organization and our responsibility to the data owners,

27
00:01:38.949 --> 00:01:41.980
we need to have a standardized incident response where

28
00:01:41.989 --> 00:01:44.910
each person knows exactly what needs to be done.

29
00:01:44.919 --> 00:01:46.260
And in what sequence,

30
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:50.870
this makes it easier to prioritize the response because each person has their

31
00:01:50.879 --> 00:01:54.459
own tasks and knows how to take care of their own responsibilities,

32
00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:57.580
then communicate appropriately with others concerned.

33
00:01:58.709 --> 00:02:02.610
Next, we need to find the appropriate containment strategy,

34
00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:05.330
identify the Attackers and how they penetrated

35
00:02:05.339 --> 00:02:07.930
our defenses and isolate the attack,

36
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:11.199
making sure it does not go any further or do additional damage

37
00:02:11.729 --> 00:02:15.889
after the incident. We identify evidence that may need to be retained.

38
00:02:15.899 --> 00:02:19.529
Then often there is an internal audit of what occurred.

39
00:02:19.789 --> 00:02:23.279
External investigation may also be required especially in

40
00:02:23.289 --> 00:02:26.490
major cyber attacks where law enforcement is involved,

41
00:02:26.929 --> 00:02:29.119
lessons learned must be documented,

42
00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:33.559
perhaps it will be found that we responded better than during a previous attack,

43
00:02:33.669 --> 00:02:37.350
but we still need to improve preparation or detection analysis.

44
00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:42.600
Often these post incident activities are subject to regulatory requirements

45
00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:45.320
and certain documentation must be submitted.

46
00:02:45.649 --> 00:02:48.070
This is especially important if the compromised

47
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.619
critical information is protected by law.